<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 8: Who is Responsible?  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/ch8-research.md):
  - Three kinds of cost, each with its own case or episode:
    labour (Meta, Sama and Facebook's moderators in Kenya),
    energy and water (the stalled Microsoft and G42 data centre at Olkaria),
    misinformation (podcast 125, AI summaries).
  - The moderators' cases are NOT decided on the merits. The chapter says so.
  - The data centre facts rest on news reports. No original statement from
    the President was found.
  - Kenya's 2026 policy (2.11.7, 3.4, page 16) names both gaps itself.
  - TODO: the TED talk transcript (not available to us), the environmental
    article, and the environmental paper Lily mentioned (no links).
  - Practise (STACK) section removed on purpose. Questions go in the exercises.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-who-is-responsible" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Who is Responsible?</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-who-is-responsible">
        <p>
            I once fell while bouldering, and went to A&amp;E.
            Afterwards I wanted to know how high the wall was.
            Google's AI summary gave a height of 22 metres.
            That didn't sound right.
            I found the venue's own article, which was dated 1 April, and
            ended with <q>Happy April Fool's Day</q>
            (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-125" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 125, n.d.</xref>).
        </p>

        <p>
            Nobody was hurt by that mistake.
            But it makes a good question.
            Who was responsible?
            The venue, for a joke that was easy to read the wrong way?
            Google, for the summary?
            Me, for believing it?
        </p>

        <p>
            In the last chapter we looked at principles.
            This chapter is about what happens when something goes wrong, and
            who pays.
            We look at three kinds of cost: misinformation, human labour, and
            energy and water.
            For each one we ask who bears the cost, and who decides.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe different types of cost of AI systems: labour,
                        energy and misinformation.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate real examples where AI led to harm or unfair
                        outcomes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Debate who should be held responsible: developers,
                        users, funders, regulators or someone else.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Propose design or policy changes that support shared
                        responsibility and transparency.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. Case study: the moderators                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-who-is-responsible-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: Who Employs the People Behind the Feed?</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-moderators-who">
            <title>The people who clean up</title>

            <p>
                Social media feeds look clean because people clean them.
                Content moderators look at the posts that are reported or
                flagged, and decide what has to come down.
                A lot of the work is disturbing.
            </p>

            <p>
                In Kenya, much of that work for Facebook was done by workers at
                Sama, the outsourcing company we met in Chapter 2.
                Sama's staff worked for Meta, the company that owns Facebook,
                but they were employed by Sama
                (<xref ref="biblio-nothias-2026" text="custom">Nothias, 2026</xref>).
                That's the whole question.
                When something goes wrong, is it Sama's responsibility, or
                Meta's?
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-moderators-cases">
            <title>The cases</title>

            <p>
                There are several cases, in Kenya's Employment and Labour
                Relations Court.
                Here is what the sources we've read tell us.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>2022.</em>
                        Daniel Motaung, described as a whistleblower, brought the first
                        case against Meta and Sama (Petition E071 of 2022).
                        In February 2023, a judge refused to remove Meta from the
                        case.
                        Meta challenged that, arguing that it did not carry on
                        business in Kenya
                        (<xref ref="biblio-nothias-2026" text="custom">Nothias, 2026</xref>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>2023.</em>
                        A second case was brought by more than 180 former
                        moderators.
                        They say that in January 2023, Meta decided to close the
                        East African hub run by Sama, and that it told the
                        new contractor, Majorel, not to hire them.
                        In June 2023, the court issued interim orders,
                        including that Facebook should provide medical,
                        psychiatric and psychological care to the moderators.
                        (One report says this case was joined with the 2022 petition,
                        and another treats them as separate)
                        (<xref ref="biblio-nothias-2026" text="custom">Nothias, 2026</xref>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>2024.</em>
                        In September 2024, the Court of Appeal rejected Meta's
                        appeal, and found that the Kenyan court had
                        jurisdiction over it
                        (<xref ref="biblio-nothias-2026" text="custom">Nothias, 2026</xref>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>April 2026.</em>
                        A report published on 27 April 2026 says the court ruled in
                        favour of 187 former moderators.
                        What it describes is an order for a Meta director to appear to
                        be cross-examined on an affidavit that Meta had filed, with a
                        hearing date to follow
                        (<xref ref="biblio-capital-moderators-2026" text="custom">Capital FM, 2026</xref>).
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Meta's position is that it didn't hire or fire the workers, and
                that Sama employed them.
                The workers say that they've had no mental health care, and
                that their situation is getting worse.
                One legal analysis says that a judge held Meta to be the
                <q>true employer</q>, partly because the moderators used
                Facebook's own software.
                It doesn't give a date for it, or say which ruling it means, and
                we couldn't confirm it
                (<xref ref="biblio-nothias-2026" text="custom">Nothias, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    Be careful with the headlines.
                    One of them said the court had <q>backed</q> the moderators.
                    The report says the court ruled in their favour, but it only
                    describes an order for a Meta director to be cross-examined, and it
                    doesn't say what was decided.
                    As far as we can confirm, no court has yet made a final ruling on
                    who is responsible.
                    What the courts have clearly settled is where the case can be
                    heard.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-moderators-power">
            <title>Who had the power?</title>

            <p>
                Think about the positions in this story.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Meta</em> is based abroad.
                        According to the workers, it decided to close the Sama hub,
                        and the work was done on Facebook's own software.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Sama</em> hires and pays the workers, but works for Meta.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>The moderators</em> do the work, and take the risk to
                        their mental health.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>The courts and the government</em> decide what
                        counts as responsibility, and whether it can be enforced
                        against a company based abroad.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Outsourcing puts a company between the decision and the
                person who lives with it.
                That's why it's hard to say who's responsible.
                It also makes it easy for each side to point at the other.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-moderators-policy">
            <title>What Kenya's policy says</title>

            <p>
                Kenya's 2026 AI policy names this exact gap.
                It lists, among the problems it needs to fix, inadequate
                protection of AI value-chain workers.
                Data annotation and content moderation workers, it says, face
                unique risks, including exposure to harmful content, insecure
                working conditions and limited labour protections.
                Existing frameworks provide insufficient safeguards for mental
                wellbeing, transparency and employer accountability
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                It proposes to look at labour conditions across the whole AI
                value chain, including data annotation and content moderation,
                and to recognise the annotation sector as a national asset
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
                As we saw in Chapter 7, the policy isn't a law yet.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-moderators-lessons">
            <title>What the case shows</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Responsibility is easy to divide and hard to pin down.
                        Each party in a chain can say that it was someone else's.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The workers who carry the cost are often the ones with the
                        least power to change things.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Courts are slow.
                        These cases have been running since 2022.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The same questions come back to Chapters 2 and 3: who
                        does the hidden work, and who answers for it?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Podcast 125: AI summaries                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-who-is-responsible-summaries">
        <title>Who Answers for the Summary?</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-who-is-responsible-summaries">
            <p>
                The cost of misinformation is easier to see.
                This section comes from a conversation between me and David
                Stern about AI summaries
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-125" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 125, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about 26 minutes long, and it's available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/125-ai-summaries/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-who-is-responsible-podcast-125">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 125: AI Summaries</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-who-is-responsible-podcast-125"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/6396/125-ai-summaries.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-apple">
            <title>Two stories</title>

            <p>
                The first story is about Apple's AI summaries of news
                articles from the BBC.
                We talk about summaries that got things wrong.
                David gives the example of one that said a leader had died when
                they hadn't, and I mention one that said a suspect had shot himself,
                when he hadn't.
                Because the summaries appeared under the BBC's name, it was the
                BBC that complained.
            </p>

            <p>
                The second story is mine, which you heard at the start of this
                chapter.
                Google's summary came with a small note saying that it might
                not be accurate.
                I found out that it was a joke.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-summaries-who">
            <title>Who is responsible?</title>

            <p>
                David calls it a really difficult question.
                The BBC can't control what the AI says under its name.
                He floats the makers of the AI as the responsible party, but doubts
                that judges would go that far.
                He expects the courts to decide, and he doubts that Apple or
                the BBC could avoid fault just by adding a disclaimer.
                And he's fairly certain that if the makers were held legally
                responsible for accuracy, almost all summaries would disappear
                overnight.
            </p>

            <p>
                I think the reader should check.
                But someone reading the news shouldn't need to check every
                summary.
                And I wouldn't blame the venue.
                I said that I guess I was the responsible one, because I read
                the summary.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-summaries-design">
            <title>Design can help</title>

            <p>
                David has some ideas.
                Websites could label April Fool's content, so that an AI
                knows what it is.
                Summaries could link to the source, so that a person can check
                it.
                Users could flag mistakes, as Google Maps asks people to confirm
                places.
            </p>

            <p>
                He also points out the problems.
                News is time-bound, so there's often no time for a
                feedback loop.
                (My question about a wall has time for repeated flags.)
                And flagging could be abused, for example by people who
                coordinate to flag accurate climate science as
                misinformation.
                So it's a design question, and he isn't sure that users would
                bother.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 3. Energy and water                                            -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-who-is-responsible-energy">
        <title>The Cost You Can't See: Energy and Water</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-olkaria">
            <title>A data centre in Kenya</title>

            <p>
                AI runs in data centres.
                One proposal in Kenya was for a geothermal-powered data
                centre at Olkaria, near Lake Naivasha.
                Microsoft and G42 announced it in 2024 as part of a $1 billion
                investment package (about KES 130 billion at the 2026 rate).
                It was to run on geothermal energy, and to grow to up to one
                gigawatt (1,000 megawatts)
                (<xref ref="biblio-aljazeera-2026" text="custom">Sheikh, 2026</xref>;
                <xref ref="biblio-techish-2026" text="custom">Tech-ish, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                In May 2026 it stalled.
                Al Jazeera says that the plans stalled over concerns about
                the available power
                (<xref ref="biblio-aljazeera-2026" text="custom">Sheikh, 2026</xref>).
                Two other outlets attribute the halt to President Ruto.
                One of them quotes him as saying that switching it on at full
                scale would mean shutting off power for <q>half the country</q>
                (<xref ref="biblio-techish-2026" text="custom">Tech-ish, 2026</xref>;
                <xref ref="biblio-thinkgeo-2026" text="custom">Cariaga, 2026</xref>).
                We couldn't find the President's original statement.
                Microsoft and G42 haven't commented publicly on the stall.
                KenGen, the power company, says the data centre is still at the
                design stage
                (<xref ref="biblio-aljazeera-2026" text="custom">Sheikh, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                To see why, look at the numbers.
                Kenya has about 3,000 megawatts of installed capacity, and nearly
                90% of it is renewable.
                Geothermal makes up 47%
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
                A campus of up to 1,000 megawatts would be about a third of
                that, and Tech-ish says the first phase would have been 100
                megawatts.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-naivasha">
            <title>The water question</title>

            <p>
                Power isn't the only worry.
                Data centres need water for cooling.
                Residents around Lake Naivasha are concerned.
                Silas Wanjala of the Lake Naivasha Riparian Association says that
                the lake nearly dried up in 2010 from over-abstraction, and that
                it could happen again.
                Grace Kimani, a local patrol leader, says that the data
                centre's water demand could add pressure in dry periods, and she
                calls for more transparency
                (<xref ref="biblio-aljazeera-2026" text="custom">Sheikh, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                No figure for the project's water use has been published, and
                Al Jazeera notes that nobody has said how much water it would
                need, where it would come from, or how it would be cooled.
                Microsoft and G42 said at the 2024 announcement that they would
                use water-conservation technology.
                Al Jazeera says its efforts to reach them and Kenyan officials for
                comment were unsuccessful
                (<xref ref="biblio-aljazeera-2026" text="custom">Sheikh, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Kenya's own policy says that large data centres can use up to 5
                million gallons of water a day for cooling, and that about 80%
                of Kenya's land is arid or semi-arid.
                It says that there are currently no requirements for disclosing or
                managing the water use of AI infrastructure, and no mandatory
                reporting on energy
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-energy-policy">
            <title>Who pays, and who decides?</title>

            <p>
                Ask the three questions.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Who gets the benefit?</em>
                        The companies that sell AI services, and possibly Kenya,
                        through jobs, investment and local compute.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Who carries the cost?</em>
                        Possibly everyone who relies on the same grid and water, and
                        in particular the people who live around a lake that, residents
                        say, nearly dried up in 2010.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Who decides?</em>
                        With no disclosure rules, it seems to be mostly the companies and
                        the government, and the public has little information.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                The 2026 policy proposes to change that.
                It would require disclosure of energy use, water use, cooling
                methods and emissions for high-impact AI infrastructure, and
                require projects to be assessed for energy, water, land and
                community impact
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">State Department for ICT and the Digital Economy, 2026</xref>).
                Again, it's a policy and not yet a law.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-ted">
            <title>Sasha Luccioni on the other costs</title>

            <p>
                The researcher Sasha Luccioni gave a TED talk in 2023.
                She argues that we should focus on the harms that are
                happening now, like carbon emissions, copyright infringement
                and biased information, instead of far-off risks.
                She suggests practical ways to make AI more inclusive and
                transparent
                (<xref ref="biblio-ted-2023" text="custom">Luccioni, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-who-is-responsible-ted">
                <caption>Sasha Luccioni, AI is dangerous, but not for the reasons you think (TED, 2023)</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-who-is-responsible-ted"
                             iframe="https://embed.ted.com/talks/sasha_luccioni_ai_is_dangerous_but_not_for_the_reasons_you_think"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="16:9"/>
            </figure>

            <!-- TODO: add a summary of the talk's figures and tools once we have
                 the transcript, and add the environmental article and the paper
                 on AI's environmental impact (links not yet supplied). -->
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 4. Podcasts 267 and 268: who pays                              -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-who-is-responsible-system">
        <title>Looking Behind the Product</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-who-is-responsible-system">
            <p>
                Two conversations between David and Kate Fleming, another
                director at IDEMS, look at the system behind AI products.
                They're part of a series that's loosely in conversation with
                Karen Hao's book <em>Empire of AI</em>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-who-is-responsible-podcast-268">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 268: What Lies Behind AI as a Product?</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-who-is-responsible-podcast-268"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/12535/268-what-lies-behind-ai-as-a-product.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-who-is-responsible-podcast-267">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 267: The Forces Shaping AI</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-who-is-responsible-podcast-267"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/12510/267-the-forces-shaping-ai.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-product">
            <title>The product and the system</title>

            <p>
                In episode 268, Kate says that Silicon Valley presents AI as a
                product, which is the output and what the user sees
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-268" text="custom">Stern and Fleming, episode 268, 2026</xref>).
                Behind it are people, resources, labour and infrastructure.
                David repeats a slogan from the Earthkeepers event, that there is
                no cloud, only Earth.
                The <q>cloud</q> is physical, in data centres and devices.
            </p>

            <p>
                Kate mentions data work done by low-paid workers, including
                labelling and moderating harmful content, and says that a
                company called Scale AI goes to places like Venezuela and Kenya
                for workers.
                David says that the digital jobs may not be great, but that they
                are valued by the people who get them.
                Kate says the problem is unfair pay and a lack of worker
                protection.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-who-is-responsible-externalities">
            <title>Who bears the cost?</title>

            <p>
                Kate's point in both episodes is about costs that a company
                doesn't have to pay.
                In 267 she says that if a business causes harm, society
                pays: we as taxpayers, communities and the environment
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-267" text="custom">Stern and Fleming, episode 267, 2026</xref>).
                She says AI is the poster child for why we need to start
                holding businesses accountable.
            </p>

            <p>
                David agrees: he says it's no longer profitable if you start to
                make people accountable.
                So the real question, he says, is whether regulation can make
                that happen, given how much has been deregulated.
            </p>

            <p>
                These are their views, and they aren't the only ones.
                But they fit the pattern in this chapter: the people who gain
                from AI aren't always the people who pay.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Illustrative example                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-who-is-responsible-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Follow the Cost</title>

        <p>
            Pick an AI tool that you use, like a chatbot, a translation tool or
            an image generator.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Write down everything that has to happen for it to give you
                    one answer.
                    Include the people, the places and the machines.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Next to each one, write who pays for it.
                    Is it you, the company, a worker, the public or the
                    environment?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find out what the company says about it, in its terms or on
                    its website.
                    Does it tell you about the labour, the energy or the water?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Would knowing change how you use it?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 6. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-who-is-responsible">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-1">
            <title>Choose two scenarios</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Pick two of the four scenarios below.
                    For each one, explain your reasoning, and think about who
                    was responsible, and who had power and who didn't.
                    What's the best way forward?
                    Refer back to earlier cases, like COMPAS, the Dutch childcare
                    scandal, SHA or the Kenyan workers, or to the principles in
                    Chapter 7.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Misinformation.</em>
                    An AI chatbot gives students false information about a
                    scholarship application deadline.
                    Several students miss the deadline and lose the
                    opportunity.
                    Who is responsible, and what should happen next?
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Fairness or progress?</em>
                    An AI system helps smallholder farmers to choose the best
                    planting times and fertilisers.
                    It performs better for large commercial farms, which had
                    more data in the training set.
                    Should it still be rolled out?
                    Is it better than nothing, or should it wait until it's more
                    inclusive?
                    Who should fix it: the developers, the ministry or the
                    users?
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Hidden bias?</em>
                    A job platform for young people uses AI to match applicants
                    with opportunities.
                    Over time it's found to favour applicants from cities, and
                    to under-represent rural and lower-income candidates.
                    Is the algorithm biased, even if location isn't an input?
                    Who should be accountable: the developers, the funders, the
                    platform or someone else?
                    What should happen next?
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Ethics and transparency.</em>
                    Your AI assistant generates text and images instantly.
                    You find out that underpaid workers reviewed offensive
                    content for months to make it safe.
                    Do users have a right to know how AI systems are trained?
                    Should companies have to disclose labour conditions?
                    Would knowing change how you use these tools?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-2">
            <title>Reflect on a real example</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose a real example, from the course or your own.
                    In no more than 500 words, respond to these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What went wrong, or what could go wrong?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who should be considered responsible, and why?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would a more responsible version of the system
                            look like?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Could transparency, human oversight or better data
                            have helped?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How do labour, energy, access or fairness come into
                            your answer?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-3">
            <title>Three kinds of cost</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe one example of each cost from this chapter, in a
                    sentence or two: labour, energy and water, and
                    misinformation.
                    For each one, say who pays, and who decides.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-4">
            <title>Pointing at each other</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the moderators' cases, Meta says that Sama employed the
                    workers.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What would make you say that Meta was the employer, in
                            practice?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Why does it matter whether a company that's based
                            abroad can be sued in Kenya?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you put in a contract between a tech
                            company and an outsourcer, to make responsibility
                            clear?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-5">
            <title>The lake and the data centre</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Imagine you're on the committee that decides whether a data
                    centre can be built near a lake that residents say nearly dried up in 2010.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What would you want to know before you decided?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Who would you want in the room?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you ask the company to publish?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How do you weigh jobs and investment against water
                            for the people who live nearby?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-6">
            <title>A disclosure rule</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Kenya's policy proposes disclosure of energy and water use for
                    large AI infrastructure.
                    Write a short disclosure rule for either that, or for the
                    working conditions of the people who label or moderate data.
                    What would the company have to say, to whom, how often, and
                    what would happen if it didn't?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-7">
            <title>Who answers for the summary?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose one of the summary stories: the BBC and Apple, or the
                    bouldering wall.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who do you think should be responsible: the
                            publisher, the company that made the summary, or the
                            reader?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            David suggests that if the makers were held legally
                            responsible, summaries might disappear.
                            Would that be a bad outcome?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Which of David's design ideas would you choose, and
                            what could go wrong with it?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-who-is-responsible-8">
            <title>Who gets the benefit?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Kate says that AI is the poster child for holding
                    businesses accountable for costs that society bears.
                    Pick a cost from this chapter and make the case for and
                    against that view.
                    What would holding the business accountable look like in
                    practice?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

